# damn-daniel design refresh POC

Reference-only Kibana design-refresh POC on `damn-daniel` — **never merge to main**. Optimize for speed and visual fidelity, not production readiness. Pair with the sibling EUI POC branch.

## Skip

- Writing or updating tests (Jest, FTR, Scout, snapshots) unless explicitly asked
- Running tests as part of implementation
- i18n string churn unless the copy change is the task
- Changelogs / docs / Storybook polish
- Deprecation paths, migration notes, backwards compatibility
- Exhaustive a11y audits
- Unrelated refactors

## Still do

- Keep typecheck and local Kibana start green
- Prefer EUI tokens and components over hardcoded styles
- Mark POC shortcuts with `// POC:`
- Keep basic keyboard focus/semantics when changing interactive UI
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` for new motion

## Scope

- Only change the surface named in the task
- Don’t touch neighboring apps “while you’re in there”
- If a design value isn’t specified, pick a reasonable default, flag with `// POC: needs design input`, and mention it in the summary

## EUI

- This branch consumes the EUI design-refresh POC via `yarn watch --kibana` or a tarball pin
- Don’t casually bump `@elastic/eui*` to published main
- After EUI sync, component markup may need a Kibana restart with `--no-cache` (shared-deps); theme token changes often show sooner
- Component/token API changes belong in the EUI sibling branch, not here

## Commits

Use: `[Area / Subarea] Succinct result-focused description.`

- Capitalize the description; end with a period
- Lead with the result, not a file list
- Title only is fine

```
# Good
[Chrome / Loading] Use primary for the global progress bar.
[Login] Apply glass panel treatment to the form card.
```

## When finishing

Summarize what changed, list `POC:` shortcuts / open design questions, and note anything that depends on the EUI POC build.
